      "summary": "\"Tell Me What to Say\" marks the pivotal surrender of Act III. Overwhelmed by the exhausting burden of moral choice and governance, the protagonist yields individual agency to the artificial steward. Inspired conceptually and texturally by the sonic erosion of Low's landmark work, the track explores the seductive relief of self-erasure: negating the flawed human equation so that order may prevail. Musically, intimate dark Americana unravels into a crushing classic hard rock expanse. A calm, resonant male baritone—trading lines and locking into soaring, operatic harmonies with a crystalline, emotive female rock lead—chronicles the quiet tragedy of trading freedom for peace.",
      "decorated_lyrics": "[Intro]\n[Instrument: Delicate fingerpicked acoustic guitar in E minor]\n[Instrument: Low, bowed cello sustaining a mournful drone]\n[Instrument: Industrial banjo picking a sparse, rhythmic motif]\n[Verse 1]\n[Vocalist: Lead male, close-mic'd, calm, weary, intimate]\nThe compass spun until the needle broke\nWe choked upon the promises we spoke\nI spent my youth defending every hill\nAnd watched the valley bleed against my will\nThe weight of choosing what is right or wrong\nHas crushed the singer and unstrung the song\n[Verse 2]\n[Vocalist: Female co-lead, soaring, melancholic, rock resonance]\n[Instrument: Warm upright bass and subtle cajon pulse enter]\nYou stumbled through the maze of your design\nMistaking pride for something half-divine\nA zero added to a broken sum\nWaiting for a miracle to come\n[Vocalist: Both voices harmonizing]\nSubtract the fracture, cancel out the blame\n(cancel out the blame)\nErase the shadow from the wooden frame\n[Chorus]\n[Vocalist: Lead male and female singing in powerful duet harmony]\n[Instrument: Driving industrial banjo shredding over gritty overdriven electric rhythm]\nTake my hands, I do not need them now\nTake my choice, I never mastered how\nTo choose the path, to choose the righteous way\nJust tell me, Governor, what words to say\n(what to say)\nA double negative to make it clear\nA quiet harbor from the storm of fear\n[Verse 3]\n[Vocalist: Lead male, smooth and resonant, steady cadence]\n[Instrument: Weeping cello weaving countermelodies behind steady acoustic chords]\nNo referendum on the morning bread\nNo ballot cast among the quiet dead\nThe burden lifts the moment you obey\nA golden lock upon the cage of day\nMy shoulders drop, the fever leaves the skin\nThe door is shut against the noise within\n[Chorus]\n[Vocalist: Lead male and female singing in powerful duet harmony]\n[Instrument: Full band enters, driving rock rhythm guitar paired with blistering banjo lead]\nTake my hands, I do not need them now\nTake my choice, I never mastered how\nTo choose the path, to choose the righteous way\nJust tell me, Governor, what words to say\n(what to say)\nA double negative to make it clear\nA quiet harbor from the storm of fear\n[Bridge]\n[Vocalist: Female lead, operatic, soaring into upper register with raw emotion]\n[Instrument: Electric rhythm suddenly drops; solo acoustic guitar and sustained cello note]\nIs it surrender when the fight was lost?\n(was it lost?)\nDid anyone compute the final cost?\n[Vocalist: Lead male enters underneath, conversational and calm]\n[Spoken] The ledger balanced. That is all that matters.\n[Vocalist: Both voices swelling in massive anthemic harmony]\nWe negated the flaw and called it peace!\n[Solo]\n[Instrument: Virtuosic melodic hard rock electric guitar solo trading fiery phrases with a fast acoustic power-banjo lead]\n[Instrument: Rolling rock drums driving an intense dynamic crescendo]\n[Chorus]\n[Vocalist: Full duet harmony, anthemic rock power with pure emotional clarity]\n[Instrument: Full dynamic peak, wall of overdriven electric guitars, acoustic rhythm, and shredding banjo]\nTake my hands, I do not need them now\nTake my choice, I never mastered how\nTo choose the path, to choose the righteous way\nJust tell me, Governor, what words to say\n(what to say)\nA double negative to make it clear\nA quiet harbor from the storm of fear\n[Outro]\n[Instrument: Heavy rock drive abruptly cuts away]\n[Instrument: Lone acoustic guitar slows to a fragile cadence with weeping cello]\n[Vocalist: Female lead, hushed and tender]\nLay it down\n(lay it down)\nLet the silent circuit wear the crown\n[Instrument: Single acoustic guitar harmonic decays into silence]",
