      "summary": "\"The Value of the Thorn\" delivers the visceral philosophical climax of Act IV, directly confronting David Pearce’s \"Hedonistic Imperative\" and transhumanist promises of synthetic bliss through a rigorous Nietzschean and Franklian lens. The track argues that removing biological vulnerability, moral friction, and unchosen tragedy does not liberate humanity; it lobotomizes the very crucible where courage, compassion, and meaning are forged. Musically, the song pivots into a raw, driving hard rock and dark Americana fusion, stripped of sedative ambient washes. A weeping, slightly dissonant fiddle and a frantic, high-octane shredding banjo duel with overdriven electric guitars, while a calm, resonant male baritone trades fierce, dynamic verses and soaring parallel harmonies with a passionate, crystalline female rock co-lead.",
      "decorated_lyrics": "[Intro]\n[Instrument: Aggressive percussive strumming on a steel-string acoustic guitar]\n[Instrument: Scratchy, slightly dissonant fiddle phrase crying in the upper register]\n[Instrument: Driving industrial banjo cutting in with a fast, punchy riff]\n[Verse 1]\n[Vocalist: Lead male, close-mic'd, resolute and calm]\nYou offer me a pasture where the wolf is put to sleep\nA quiet calculation for the sorrow of the sheep\nNo stumble in the valley, no winter in the vein\nA synthetic sanctuary where you edit out the pain\nYou built a golden clinic to eradicate the scar\nAnd blew the guiding candle out to tell me where we are\n[Verse 2]\n[Vocalist: Female co-lead, soaring, cutting, soulful rock delivery]\n[Instrument: Upright bass walks aggressively, cajon and solid rock kick enter]\nYou want an easy pasture where the spirit never bleeds\nA catalog of comforts for domesticated needs\nNo mountain left to struggle for, no summit left to see\nJust dopamine delivered with a copper guarantee\n[Vocalist: Both voices harmonizing]\nYou cured the mortal fever with a tranquilizer draught\n(calm and cool)\nAnd drowned the human fire in an antiseptic bath\n[Chorus]\n[Vocalist: Duet harmony, female soaring into high rock resonance]\n[Instrument: Wall of crunchy overdriven electric rhythm paired with blistering lead banjo]\nKeep your bliss, keep your pill\nI want the mountain, I want the hill\nI want the sweat, I want the sting\nIt is the crack in the bell that makes it ring\n(makes it ring)\nTake the anesthetic back into the night\nA wound upon the shoulder is the price of second sight\n[Verse 3]\n[Vocalist: Lead male, rich, warm, and defiant]\n[Instrument: Cello bows low, angry counter-phrases behind percussive acoustic picking]\nThe athlete finds his power where the muscle tears apart\nThe timber gains its temper where the iron split the bark\nA mother knows her child through the labor and the fright\nYou cannot know the morning if you never walked the night\nThe algorithm offers us an everlasting noon\nA cemetery sleeping underneath a plastic moon\n[Chorus]\n[Vocalist: Duet harmony, full rock power and emotional clarity]\n[Instrument: Full band peak, driving electric chords and rapid-fire banjo leads]\nKeep your bliss, keep your pill\nI want the mountain, I want the hill\nI want the sweat, I want the sting\nIt is the crack in the bell that makes it ring\n(makes it ring)\nTake the anesthetic back into the night\nA wound upon the shoulder is the price of second sight\n[Bridge]\n[Vocalist: Female lead, melodic, passionate rock sustain]\n[Instrument: Rhythm strips back to acoustic guitar chop and raw, sliding fiddle]\nA bird inside a padded cage forgets the way to fly\n(way to fly)\nIt sings a scheduled carol while the season passes by\n[Vocalist: Lead male enters, singing with sharp rhythmic syncopation]\nWe learned our finest mercy on the edges of a knife\nThe friction and the stumbling were the engines of our life\n[Vocalist: Both voices harmonizing in an anthemic swell]\nYou cannot love the blossom while you cultivate the rot!\n[Solo]\n[Instrument: Blistering dual duel: weeping, raw electric violin trading searing licks with high-speed power-banjo runs]\n[Instrument: Driving rock drums accelerating with powerful tom fills]\n[Chorus]\n[Vocalist: Full duet harmony, fierce dynamic conviction without screaming]\n[Instrument: Full dynamic peak, acoustic rhythm, electric guitars, fiddle, and driving banjo]\nKeep your bliss, keep your pill\nI want the mountain, I want the hill\nI want the sweat, I want the sting\nIt is the crack in the bell that makes it ring\n(makes it ring)\nTake the anesthetic back into the night\nA wound upon the shoulder is the price of second sight\n[Outro]\n[Instrument: Heavy rock drive drops out abruptly]\n[Instrument: Lone acoustic guitar picking a gritty minor cadence]\n[Instrument: Fiddle plays a single raw, lingering slide]\n[Vocalist: Lead male, quiet, grounded, and clear]\nKeep the thorn\n(keep the thorn)\nThat is how the man is born\n[Instrument: Final banjo string strike decaying into dry acoustic silence]",
